export const HELP = {
  import: {
    title: 'Import the book',
    html: "<p>Bring the book in from an epub or text file so ReadToMe has something to read. Creating a project stores the file; reading it in splits it into volumes, parts, chapters and paragraphs, which every later step works on.</p>\n<h3>How</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>On the Projects page, choose <strong>New project</strong>. Fill in <strong>Book title</strong>, <strong>Project title</strong> and <strong>Author</strong>, then drop the book on <strong>Drop the book here</strong> (epub or txt, up to 100 MB) and choose <strong>Create</strong>. The project title names the project folder and follows the book title until you change it. The book title is the one the finished audiobook carries.</li>\n<li>On the Overview, choose <strong>Read book</strong> on the Import row. An epub is split by its own table of contents. A text file comes in as one chapter, which the Structure step can split.</li>\n<li>While you are on the Overview, add a cover on the project card (<strong>Add a cover</strong>: JPG, PNG or WebP, up to 10 MB). The cover is embedded in the exported audiobook. The same card edits the title and author.</li>\n<li>If the book is narrated entirely by one voice, with no dialog to cast, turn on <strong>Narrator only</strong>: only narration then gets audio.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Reread… (auto splits)</strong> reads the source file in again from scratch. It deletes everything made since: attribution, audio, edits, inserted titles and pauses, and your confirmed and skipped steps. Use it only to start over.</p>\n<h3>Done when</h3>\n<p>The Import row reads <strong>Done</strong> and shows how many items the book was read into.</p>\n",
  },
  structure: {
    title: 'Sort the structure',
    html: "<p>Check that parts, chapters and headings came through as the book has them, and fix what the import got wrong. Chapter markers in the audiobook, spoken titles and pauses all follow this structure, so sort it before the later steps.</p>\n<h3>How</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Choose <strong>Check structure</strong> to open the Book page. The tree on the left lists every volume, part and chapter. Look for a book that came in as one chapter, chapters that are far too long or short, a heading such as “Chapter 12” left as an ordinary paragraph, and missing or wrong titles. An untitled node shows as “Untitled chapter 3”.</li>\n<li>Fix a few problems by hand from a node's menu (the ⋮ beside it in the tree or on its heading):\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Split chapter</strong> on a paragraph starts a new chapter there. <strong>Split part</strong> and <strong>Split volume</strong> do the same one level up.</li>\n<li><strong>Merge with previous</strong> and <strong>Merge with next</strong> fold two neighbours together.</li>\n<li><strong>Insert chapter after</strong> and its siblings add an empty node. <strong>Delete chapter</strong> removes one with all it holds.</li>\n<li><strong>Edit title</strong> renames a volume, part or chapter.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>When the chapter headings are plain lines in the text, use <strong>Reread with split rules…</strong> instead. Say whether the book has volumes and parts. Then, for each level, choose how a heading line looks: <strong>Text prefix</strong> (for example “Chapter”), <strong>Number (Arabic)</strong> or <strong>Roman numerals</strong>. The matching line becomes the title. This reread deletes attribution, audio, titles, pauses and your confirmed steps, so do it first.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Done when</h3>\n<p>The tree matches the book. Choose <strong>Confirm</strong> on the Structure row, or <strong>Skip</strong> when the import was already right. Splitting, merging, inserting or deleting a chapter-level node later marks the step as changed, so you can confirm it again.</p>\n",
  },
  speakers: {
    title: 'Name the speakers',
    html: "<p>Discover the book's characters and give every line of speech a speaker. Narration belongs to the Narrator. Each quoted line needs the character who says it, because that character's voice will read it.</p>\n<h3>How</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Optionally, on the Cast page, choose <strong>Discover</strong> to have the LLM suggest the cast with their aliases. Untick anyone you don't want and choose <strong>Add selected</strong>. Attribution also creates characters as it meets them, so this step is optional; it helps most when the main characters have several names.</li>\n<li>Choose <strong>Attribute unprocessed</strong> on the Speakers row. The LLM assigns a speaker, and a delivery cue, to each dialog line. Progress shows in Activity. A paragraph it could not settle shows <strong>Failed</strong> or <strong>Unknown</strong>.</li>\n<li>Review in <strong>Speakers</strong> mode on the Book page. Click a line's speaker chip to pick another character, clear it, or add a <strong>New character…</strong>. To fix many at once, tick paragraphs and use <strong>Bulk assign speaker</strong>, or <strong>Attribute</strong> to run the LLM on just those again.</li>\n<li>When quoted speech was read in as narration, use <strong>Release narration</strong> on the paragraph, chapter or part menu. It turns those paragraphs into dialog that needs a speaker.</li>\n<li>Tidy the cast. When one person appears under two names, open the extra one and choose <strong>Merge</strong>, keeping its name as an alias. Add other names under <strong>Aliases</strong> so later attribution finds the right character. For a first-person book, set <strong>Narrated by</strong> on the Narrator row.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Changing the speaker of a line that already has audio discards that audio.</p>\n<h3>Done when</h3>\n<p>No paragraphs are unassigned and you have read through the speakers. Then choose <strong>Confirm review</strong>. If new unassigned paragraphs appear later, the step asks for attention again.</p>\n",
  },
  text: {
    title: 'Polish the text',
    html: "<p>Fix the words a narrator would stumble over before any audio is made. Leftovers from print, such as page numbers, footnote markers, running headers, words broken across lines and all-caps headings, are read aloud exactly as they appear.</p>\n<h3>How</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Choose <strong>Review text</strong> and read the book on the Book page.</li>\n<li>Fix one line by hand in <strong>Speakers</strong> mode: open the item's menu and choose <strong>Edit text</strong>. For a volume, part or chapter heading, use <strong>Edit title</strong> on its menu.</li>\n<li>For a pattern that repeats, choose <strong>Edit with AI…</strong>. Describe the change in plain words, for example “make the all-caps headings sentence case”, then choose <strong>Analyze</strong> and <strong>Generate proposals</strong>. Review each proposed change. Click a row to correct it by hand, or use <strong>Ask AI again</strong> with a hint. Then <strong>Apply</strong> the ones you keep. Edit with AI changes titles and paragraph text only, and never deletes text.</li>\n<li>For a name or word the voice says wrong, use <strong>Pronunciations</strong>. Select the word in the text, choose <strong>Add pronunciation</strong>, and give a <strong>Spoken form</strong>, for example “KULL-gun” for “Kulgan”. It applies everywhere the word appears. Play <strong>This sentence</strong> to hear it.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Each of these edits discards the audio of the items it changes, so polish before generating audio.</p>\n<h3>Done when</h3>\n<p>The text reads cleanly aloud. Choose <strong>Confirm</strong> on the Text row, or <strong>Skip</strong> when the book needs no changes. A later Edit with AI change marks the step as changed.</p>\n",
  },
  voices: {
    title: 'Choose the voices',
    html: "<p>Give each speaker a voice so the book sounds like its cast. A voice is either designed from a text description or cloned from a short recording. Either way it needs audio before it can read any lines.</p>\n<h3>How</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Choose <strong>Open cast</strong>. For the whole cast at once, choose <strong>Generate voice prompts</strong>: the LLM writes a voice description for every character. Then choose <strong>Generate audio</strong> to make each prompt voice's sample. Both run in Activity. If some characters already have voices, choose <strong>Only characters without voices</strong>. <strong>Clear and regenerate all</strong> deletes every existing voice first.</li>\n<li>To shape one character's voice, open it and use <strong>Add voice</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Prompt</strong>: write or edit the <strong>Voice description prompt</strong> (or <strong>Regenerate with AI</strong>), choose <strong>Save prompt</strong>, then <strong>Generate audio</strong>. Listen, and regenerate until it fits.</li>\n<li><strong>Reference audio</strong>: upload a clean recording of 30 s or less. 15 s or less clones best. Under <strong>Transcript</strong>, <strong>Send to AI</strong> writes down what the recording says, which helps some providers clone it. <strong>Edit audio</strong> offers clean-up steps such as <strong>Denoise</strong> and <strong>Silence trim</strong>. Preview a step before you <strong>Apply</strong> it.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Mark the voice to use with the star (<strong>Set as default</strong>).</li>\n<li>When a character's voice should change partway through the book, for example a child who grows up, add a second voice and use <strong>Add rule</strong>. Pick the voice, an anchor (volume, part, chapter, paragraph or line), and <strong>From here on</strong> or <strong>Just this node</strong>. <strong>Resolved voice per chapter</strong> shows the result. A rule whose anchor was deleted by a reread or a structure edit is skipped and flagged.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Changing a voice does not regenerate lines already spoken in it.</p>\n<h3>Done when</h3>\n<p>Every speaker with lines has a voice with audio. The Voices row reads <strong>Done</strong>.</p>\n",
  },
  titles: {
    title: 'Set the titles',
    html: "<p>Give every chapter the title the listener will hear and see. Chapter markers in the audiobook always use the node titles. A listener only hears a title if a spoken title paragraph is inserted for it.</p>\n<h3>How</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Make the titles right first. In the Book page tree, use <strong>Edit title</strong> on any volume, part or chapter that is untitled or wrong. Edit with AI can rename many at once, for example “rename every chapter to 'Chapter {n}'”. Check the book title and author on the Overview's project card too.</li>\n<li>Choose <strong>Add titles…</strong>. Tick the levels to announce: <strong>Book title</strong>, <strong>Volume titles</strong>, <strong>Part titles</strong>, <strong>Chapter titles</strong>. A level the book doesn't have (one volume, say) is left out. Then choose <strong>Add titles</strong>. Each ticked level gets a spoken title paragraph at its start, read by the Narrator.</li>\n<li>Add titles once. Running it again inserts a second copy. An inserted title is ordinary text, so a later <strong>Edit title</strong> does not change it: edit or delete the paragraph itself. A reread removes inserted titles.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Spoken titles are narration, so they need audio like any other line.</p>\n<h3>Done when</h3>\n<p>Every node has the title you want and the spoken titles are in. Choose <strong>Confirm</strong> on the Titles row, or <strong>Skip</strong> for a book that needs no spoken titles. Renaming a node or changing the chapter structure later marks the step as changed.</p>\n",
  },
  pauses: {
    title: 'Add the pauses',
    html: "<p>Decide where silence belongs between parts, chapters and scenes. Without pauses, the audiobook runs each paragraph straight into the next, and one chapter straight into the next.</p>\n<h3>How</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Choose <strong>Add pauses</strong> and confirm. It inserts a <strong>Paragraph pause</strong> between paragraphs in every chapter. At the end of each chapter it inserts a <strong>Chapter pause</strong>, or a <strong>Part pause</strong> or <strong>Volume pause</strong> where a part or volume ends. Where a pause already exists it adds none.</li>\n<li>Add one-off gaps by hand, such as a scene break. In <strong>Speakers</strong> or <strong>Audio</strong> mode, open an item's menu and choose <strong>Insert pause before</strong> or <strong>Insert pause after</strong>, then pick the kind. <strong>Delete pause</strong> on a pause row removes it. Removing pauses later means deleting each one, so add them once the structure is settled. A reread removes them all.</li>\n<li>Set how long each kind lasts in Settings, <strong>Audio processing</strong>, under <strong>Pause durations</strong>. The defaults are Volume 4000 ms, Part 3000 ms, Chapter 2500 ms, Paragraph 800 ms and Pause 500 ms. Durations apply when the audiobook is assembled, so you can change them at any time without regenerating anything.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Pauses are silence added at assembly. They need no audio of their own.</p>\n<h3>Done when</h3>\n<p>The book has pauses, and the Pauses row reads <strong>Done</strong>. Choose <strong>Skip</strong> if you want the book to run on without any.</p>\n",
  },
  audio: {
    title: 'Generate the audio',
    html: "<p>Synthesise every paragraph and check the lines that came out wrong. Each item is spoken in its speaker's voice, then transcribed and compared with the text so mistakes are flagged for you.</p>\n<h3>How</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Choose <strong>Generate needed audio</strong>. It queues every item that has a speaker but no audio. Progress, rate and ETA show in Activity. To make audio for one chapter, use <strong>Generate audio for this node</strong> on its menu in <strong>Audio</strong> mode. For chosen items, tick them and use <strong>Generate audio</strong>. Generating over existing audio replaces it.</li>\n<li>Choose <strong>Open in Audio mode</strong> to listen. The tree marks chapters with paragraphs needing audio or audio to review.</li>\n<li>Work through the flagged items:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Verify failed</strong>: the transcript differs too much from the text. Hover the chip to see the word error rate. Listen, then use <strong>Generate audio</strong> for another take, or <strong>Dismiss</strong> if it sounds right.</li>\n<li><strong>Normalize failed</strong>: post-processing failed. Check the ffmpeg path.</li>\n<li><strong>Failed</strong> or <strong>Unfinished</strong>: generation itself failed. Use <strong>Retry</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Spot-check unflagged lines too. Verification catches wrong words, not a wrong tone.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Editing text, a delivery cue, a pronunciation or a speaker discards the affected audio. Run <strong>Generate needed audio</strong> again afterwards.</p>\n<p>The result depends on Settings: the active <strong>Paragraph TTS</strong> provider, and under <strong>Audio processing</strong> the ffmpeg path, <strong>Silence trim</strong>, <strong>Consonant soften</strong>, the <strong>WER threshold</strong> (0.15 by default) and <strong>Max audio attempts</strong> (1, so no automatic retry).</p>\n<h3>Done when</h3>\n<p>No paragraphs are missing audio and the Audio row reads <strong>Done</strong>.</p>\n",
  },
  export: {
    title: 'Export the audiobook',
    html: "<p>Assemble the finished audio into one audiobook file with chapters and cover art. The result is a single <code>.m4b</code> with one chapter marker per volume, part and chapter. It carries the book title, the author and the project's cover.</p>\n<h3>How</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Choose <strong>Assemble</strong> to open the Export page. Under <strong>Readiness</strong>, check how many items have audio and that ffmpeg is found. Assembly needs only ffmpeg, not the TTS or LLM services. Set its path in Settings, <strong>Audio processing</strong>, and use <strong>Test ffmpeg</strong> to check it.</li>\n<li>Choose <strong>Assemble</strong>. Progress runs through <strong>Gather</strong>, <strong>Silence</strong>, <strong>Probe &amp; concat</strong>, <strong>Encode</strong> and <strong>Finalize</strong>. <strong>Cancel</strong> stops it. Only one project assembles at a time.</li>\n<li>If some items still lack audio, ReadToMe offers a partial build. It leaves those items out and saves the file with <code>_partial_</code> and the date in its name. Use it to listen to a draft, then finish the audio for the real build.</li>\n<li>Download the file from <strong>Outputs</strong>. Builds are kept in the project's <code>output</code> folder. A full build is named after the book title, and a new full build replaces the previous one.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>After any change that needs new audio, generate it on the Audio step, then assemble again. Changing pause durations or the cover only needs a new assembly.</p>\n<h3>Done when</h3>\n<p>A full build exists. The Export row shows <strong>Done</strong> with the date of the last build.</p>\n",
  },
} as const satisfies Record<PipelineStepId, HelpEntry>;
